  "rfc": "232",
  "date": "2026-08-16",
  "reason": "RFC-232 exact-ordinal FieldValue/QOV resolution changes plan RENDERING, so the plan-shape hash and the dedup key derived from it move for 245 of 8150 scenarios across 38 family files. Two causes, both measured. (1) A sort key is now rendered from the Value the plan evaluates rather than from the spelling it was constructed with, so it prints as _current.NAME#ordinal; the old text carried a MINTED correlation name off a global counter and was not even reproducible between runs. (2) A nested field read on an outer joins null-supplying leg now reanchors onto the joined carrier instead of staying rooted on the leg. ROWS ARE UNCHANGED and that is checked twice: the re-bless tool recomputes the candidate, all four renderings, the schema, the setup and the frozen rows and errors on any difference in them, and TestFDB_FactoryCorpusFull executes the whole re-blessed corpus against a real cluster. The drift also concealed a real defect this change fixes: cause (2) did not happen at all before it, so a nested read on a null-supplying leg under an ORDER BY spanning both join sides failed at runtime with `exact QOV \"L\" ... has no declared runtime binding` - the producer bridges nested-prefix arm demanded byte-equal root types while a null-supplying leg is spelled NULLABLE by its producer and NOT NULL by a consumer that inherited the legs own row. Six committed scenarios reported it; TestFDB_OuterJoinNestedFieldBinding and TestReanchorCrossesANullabilityWidenedLegRootIntoANestedPath pin it.",
